1
00:00:19,097 --> 00:00:22,460
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I was thinking about a question.

2
00:00:25,323 --> 00:00:42,978
[SPEAKER_00]: Suppose you kind of recognize the degree to which there's nothing whatsoever that can be safely... that you can care about safely.

3
00:00:45,228 --> 00:00:47,950
[SPEAKER_00]: But, your mind still cares about things.

4
00:00:49,331 --> 00:00:51,773
[SPEAKER_00]: And wants to... like, wants to care about things.

5
00:00:55,536 --> 00:01:00,160
[SPEAKER_00]: And I'm not talking about, like, there could be good things that you care about.

6
00:01:02,201 --> 00:01:04,763
[SPEAKER_00]: So, what should be done and what should not be done?

7
00:01:06,044 --> 00:01:08,226
[SPEAKER_01]: So, gotta ask, what are the good things?

8
00:01:08,867 --> 00:01:10,168
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you mean, more specifically?

9
00:01:11,629 --> 00:01:14,411
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean things like, I don't know,

10
00:01:16,653 --> 00:01:25,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Helping other people, being reconciled with people you might have hurt in the past, family, that sort of good thing, I mean...

11
00:01:25,485 --> 00:01:33,796
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you say the mind wants to care about it, does it mean it doesn't care, but you think you should care, or it means it cares, but you're finding a problem with it?

12
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:46,942
[SPEAKER_00]: It cares, but, like, you know that you have to abandon care for even these good things.

13
00:01:47,524 --> 00:01:47,724
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

14
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:58,988
[SPEAKER_00]: And, like, at some point, you know, that's... at some point you have to stop caring about everything.

15
00:01:59,548 --> 00:02:01,810
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can... Yeah, yeah.

16
00:02:01,850 --> 00:02:04,812
[SPEAKER_01]: Colloquially speaking, like, you can't stop caring.

17
00:02:04,832 --> 00:02:05,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

18
00:02:05,453 --> 00:02:09,856
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind can just lose interest through becoming dispassionate.

19
00:02:10,336 --> 00:02:10,537
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

20
00:02:10,957 --> 00:02:12,038
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's really the question.

21
00:02:13,919 --> 00:02:18,223
[SPEAKER_01]: To what degree mind still cares for things, including good things?

22
00:02:19,730 --> 00:02:23,412
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's when you recognize that, okay, so, you can't tell it to stop.

23
00:02:23,633 --> 00:02:23,833
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

24
00:02:24,533 --> 00:02:26,414
[SPEAKER_01]: You can psychologize it as much as you want.

25
00:02:27,375 --> 00:02:30,717
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the question is then, why doesn't your mind obey you?

26
00:02:31,837 --> 00:02:34,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it not completely pliable to turn away completely from things?

27
00:02:35,099 --> 00:02:35,739
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why is it?

28
00:02:36,120 --> 00:02:36,880
[SPEAKER_01]: Why do you think it is?

29
00:02:38,321 --> 00:02:41,823
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would the mind not be completely pliable, even in regard to those subtle things?

30
00:02:43,067 --> 00:02:44,748
[SPEAKER_00]: There's two...

31
00:02:44,768 --> 00:02:46,069
[SPEAKER_00]: I think that there's two ways.

32
00:02:47,150 --> 00:03:00,019
[SPEAKER_00]: One is, like, the reason that the whole gradual training is there, is that, yeah, exactly, you can't just tell your mind to stop caring about things.

33
00:03:00,060 --> 00:03:03,142
[SPEAKER_00]: You need to kind of develop, let's say, certain

34
00:03:04,734 --> 00:03:18,699
[SPEAKER_00]: Like mental strength, like develop virtue, develop central strength, develop the ability to kind of relinquish things, or relinquish care in regard to things.

35
00:03:18,719 --> 00:03:23,301
[SPEAKER_00]: So that has to be done through the gradual training.

36
00:03:23,321 --> 00:03:25,462
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no other way to do it.

37
00:03:26,382 --> 00:03:28,423
[SPEAKER_00]: And that kind of involves

38
00:03:31,110 --> 00:03:39,633
[SPEAKER_00]: Doing it, doing the gradual training, while still seeing that, like, there's always has to be further to go, basically.

39
00:03:39,713 --> 00:03:42,173
[SPEAKER_00]: There's always more of it that you can do.

40
00:03:42,753 --> 00:03:50,636
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you just have to then stick to the gradual training and use the presence of these things as a criteria for there is still work to be done.

41
00:03:51,016 --> 00:03:52,196
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly, yeah.

42
00:03:52,676 --> 00:03:54,297
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, I don't deal with them directly,

43
00:03:54,991 --> 00:04:08,457
[SPEAKER_01]: I deal with it through gradual training, such as virtue, precepts, sense restraint, and then watchfulness of my intention on a day-to-day basis, not giving rise, not acting towards distractions on a day-to-day basis.

44
00:04:08,858 --> 00:04:16,381
[SPEAKER_01]: And all of that is making the mind more pliable, generally, making the mind more dispassionate, making the mind turn away from things on its own.

45
00:04:16,902 --> 00:04:19,683
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you recognize, oh, the things I

46
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:24,323
[SPEAKER_01]: care about are still there, but there is no care.

47
00:04:25,024 --> 00:04:29,989
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that was my second question, was basically, what constitutes caring about things?

48
00:04:31,690 --> 00:04:34,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Is the experience of care pleasant or unpleasant?

49
00:04:38,117 --> 00:04:39,678
[SPEAKER_01]: Fundamentally, unpleasant.

50
00:04:39,858 --> 00:04:41,180
[SPEAKER_01]: In its core, it's unpleasant.

51
00:04:41,240 --> 00:04:42,461
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not quite agreeable.

52
00:04:43,912 --> 00:04:45,073
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what is then care?

53
00:04:45,394 --> 00:04:56,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it presence of thoughts, perceptions about people that are close to you, that you know, that you feel like you owe them help and so on?

54
00:04:56,926 --> 00:04:59,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Or is it you being affected by the displeasure of it?

55
00:05:00,897 --> 00:05:09,562
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's... exactly, it's... you can have... you can still help, or you can still do things, let's say, or you can still have perception...

56
00:05:09,582 --> 00:05:14,545
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can still care in that sense, but not really care, because you're not affected on the emotional level.

57
00:05:14,585 --> 00:05:19,108
[SPEAKER_00]: Being detached from the things that you're doing, or the people that you would... Yeah, simply not feel it.

58
00:05:19,808 --> 00:05:20,148
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

59
00:05:20,228 --> 00:05:22,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Not feel that discomfort on account of it.

60
00:05:22,930 --> 00:05:36,973
[SPEAKER_01]: You can still care, you can look after dogs, you can look after people, you can give... you can... you can spend all night not sleeping if they need help, if they're ill, but you won't feel that discomfort of care, my care, my burden.

61
00:05:37,892 --> 00:05:38,712
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the difference.

62
00:05:39,112 --> 00:05:46,915
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why I'm highlighting the difference is because, obviously, people can easily, again, slot into a view that, okay, so I must stop caring, the mind must stop caring.

63
00:05:46,935 --> 00:05:51,656
[SPEAKER_01]: That means I will not even recognize such thoughts of what used to be my care.

64
00:05:51,716 --> 00:05:51,876
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

65
00:05:52,116 --> 00:05:53,257
[SPEAKER_01]: That can equally remain.

66
00:05:53,857 --> 00:06:06,921
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you had, like, anāgāmīs, almost virtual arahants, basically, in a household life back in the Buddha's day, when they were looking after the parents and so on, and caring for other people, but not really caring, in that sense.

67
00:06:07,743 --> 00:06:20,790
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't need to abolish the perception of duty, of, you know, paying back what you owe in terms of service and help and so on, on account of not feeling the discomfort of it.

68
00:06:21,011 --> 00:06:31,196
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that pliability of the mind, where basically you stop feeling, because the mind is not perturbed even by things such as care, such as duty.

69
00:06:31,617 --> 00:06:37,500
[SPEAKER_01]: So... and that's... that's exactly... that's not something that can be done on that intentional level.

70
00:06:40,030 --> 00:06:41,151
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how do you stop caring?

71
00:06:41,171 --> 00:06:45,152
[SPEAKER_01]: It should really be rephrased as to how do I stop being moved by care?

72
00:06:45,733 --> 00:06:49,474
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I stop feeling anything on account of even good care?

73
00:06:50,915 --> 00:06:57,998
[SPEAKER_01]: And you stop that by making the mind pliable to that extent where there is no more passion even to those little degrees.

74
00:06:58,938 --> 00:07:02,800
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no more wanting there left.

75
00:07:04,180 --> 00:07:06,221
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but we still haven't answered the first question though.

76
00:07:06,341 --> 00:07:08,382
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why is then the mind not pliable?

77
00:07:09,303 --> 00:07:13,606
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, we answered it broadly because the graduate training is not developed fully, but specifically then?

78
00:07:15,046 --> 00:07:22,091
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you then make the mind that's not sufficiently pliable, how would you make it even more pliable?

79
00:07:22,111 --> 00:07:24,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Do you take on more precepts or something?

80
00:07:25,072 --> 00:07:25,313
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

81
00:07:25,673 --> 00:07:27,714
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you keep the precepts, that doesn't change?

82
00:07:28,414 --> 00:07:29,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Guiding of the sense doors?

83
00:07:31,296 --> 00:07:33,257
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you do more of that or less of that?

84
00:07:36,839 --> 00:07:37,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Can that be improved?

85
00:07:43,532 --> 00:07:54,714
[SPEAKER_00]: I would say it's more about... it's not about adding specifically, okay, it's precepts, or it's more like guarding sense doors, or it's more this, or it's more that.

86
00:07:54,754 --> 00:08:05,497
[SPEAKER_00]: It's just, like, that these things have to be complete and unbroken, basically.

87
00:08:05,877 --> 00:08:08,937
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's not about doing... doing more of certain things.

88
00:08:08,977 --> 00:08:11,958
[SPEAKER_00]: It's more about, like, not... not allowing

89
00:08:13,985 --> 00:08:15,566
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not quantity, it's quality.

90
00:08:15,586 --> 00:08:17,047
[SPEAKER_00]: It's quality, yeah, it's not about...

91
00:08:17,527 --> 00:08:18,448
[SPEAKER_01]: But it could be both.

92
00:08:19,248 --> 00:08:24,451
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, okay, you don't need to add more precepts and so on, but you could start refining the principles behind.

93
00:08:25,612 --> 00:08:31,715
[SPEAKER_01]: Principles, like, yeah, I'm guarding my sense doors, but then start refining even that.

94
00:08:31,835 --> 00:08:40,600
[SPEAKER_01]: Not in a sense like, okay, so I will not move, I will not speak, I will not blink, I will not this or that, but it's like, no, when I do choose to do intentional acts within what I deem

95
00:08:41,222 --> 00:08:43,123
[SPEAKER_01]: To be already guided sends doors.

96
00:08:43,263 --> 00:08:45,964
[SPEAKER_01]: They don't go towards greed, towards aversion, towards distraction.

97
00:08:46,884 --> 00:08:51,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, can I refine now discernment of those intentions within that?

98
00:08:51,606 --> 00:08:55,367
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there might be some distraction still going on, to some extent.

99
00:08:56,128 --> 00:09:02,410
[SPEAKER_01]: There might be still some ill will going on, as in, not going on arising, but I'm still choosing to act at least to some degree.

100
00:09:03,150 --> 00:09:04,031
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you refine there.

101
00:09:04,651 --> 00:09:10,393
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you do kind of do more in that sense, but within what you're doing, not kind of adding more in those, like,

102
00:09:11,222 --> 00:09:16,083
[SPEAKER_01]: In a form of external behaviors and observances.

103
00:09:16,204 --> 00:09:27,627
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's what always comes down to, like, sure, you might not be engaging with coarse actions of... rooted in lust or anger or distraction, but am I engaging in subtle ones?

104
00:09:28,745 --> 00:09:32,971
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I engaging in subtle forms of distraction or desire?

105
00:09:33,031 --> 00:09:35,735
[SPEAKER_01]: That, as I said, engaging in means acting out.

106
00:09:35,895 --> 00:09:38,459
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, do I have these fleeting thoughts that pass?

107
00:09:38,539 --> 00:09:39,380
[SPEAKER_01]: They are not my problem.

108
00:09:39,941 --> 00:09:43,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Problem is when they linger around and I choose to welcome and act upon them.

109
00:09:44,087 --> 00:09:44,427
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

110
00:09:44,527 --> 00:09:49,050
[SPEAKER_00]: I act even... and I mean, that's why I asked about this, like, caring.

111
00:09:49,210 --> 00:09:57,695
[SPEAKER_00]: It's the only reason that, let's see, you can have a fleeting thought that arises, but even then, oh, let me think about this now.

112
00:09:58,015 --> 00:09:59,756
[SPEAKER_00]: That constitutes acting, in a way.

113
00:09:59,776 --> 00:10:00,857
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, it could be.

114
00:10:00,897 --> 00:10:01,898
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

115
00:10:02,218 --> 00:10:03,479
[SPEAKER_01]: Let me think about this now.

116
00:10:03,659 --> 00:10:04,940
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another fleeting thought.

117
00:10:05,380 --> 00:10:08,702
[SPEAKER_01]: But then you can also say, okay, but before I think about this now, why?

118
00:10:09,202 --> 00:10:09,622
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

119
00:10:10,103 --> 00:10:11,704
[SPEAKER_01]: I need to know where my intention is rooted.

120
00:10:11,724 --> 00:10:13,505
[SPEAKER_01]: So, why do I want to think about it now?

121
00:10:14,246 --> 00:10:22,292
[SPEAKER_01]: Is it because I already felt that fleeting discomfort, that now I just try to explain, justify, get rid of, make it fit, sort it out?

122
00:10:23,112 --> 00:10:27,135
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, I'm acting out of trying to get rid of the pain, however fleeting it might be.

123
00:10:27,295 --> 00:10:30,417
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you don't do it for that reason, because you recognize where the intention is rooted.

124
00:10:32,739 --> 00:10:33,540
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's the...

125
00:10:37,453 --> 00:10:42,739
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, that's the thing, too, that I think is... At one point, you mentioned it as you were talking there.

126
00:10:46,043 --> 00:10:50,128
[SPEAKER_00]: Because, again, there's things that are just, you know, eh...

127
00:10:53,229 --> 00:10:58,671
[SPEAKER_00]: It's things that are taken for granted as, oh, yeah, this is a good thing to... this is worth thinking about.

128
00:10:58,691 --> 00:11:00,011
[SPEAKER_00]: This is worth...

129
00:11:00,352 --> 00:11:08,514
[SPEAKER_00]: But the only reason it's taken for granted like that is, again, because there is already a care in regards to it.

130
00:11:08,734 --> 00:11:12,056
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just even to question or to kind of...

131
00:11:13,416 --> 00:11:16,778
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, wait a second, this isn't necessarily worth thinking about.

132
00:11:17,178 --> 00:11:22,702
[SPEAKER_00]: And the only reason you want to think about it is because you want to think about it, actually.

133
00:11:23,122 --> 00:11:24,983
[SPEAKER_00]: And because it pertains to... it's yours.

134
00:11:25,483 --> 00:11:28,005
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's like, oh, it's important to me.

135
00:11:28,585 --> 00:11:31,327
[SPEAKER_00]: Ah, that has to be kind of relinquished.

136
00:11:31,547 --> 00:11:39,491
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, sometimes the mind maybe is already pliable enough, but you're just overlooking exactly the immediate presence of care.

137
00:11:40,652 --> 00:11:45,635
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just overlooking the fact that what you really want to do there is just get rid of that discomfort.

138
00:11:46,515 --> 00:11:49,136
[SPEAKER_01]: And once you recognize that, you stop caring.

139
00:11:49,837 --> 00:11:51,818
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, in that sense, you stop being moved by it.

140
00:11:52,838 --> 00:12:00,662
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't need to deny the thoughts of care and help and so on, but... Oh, no, the only reason I was still pressured by care is because, to that extent, I haven't understood it.

141
00:12:01,743 --> 00:12:09,427
[SPEAKER_01]: I haven't understood the phenomenon of care, whether it's actually rooted in simply that discomfort, subtle one, but still uncomfortable, and I wanted to deal with it.

142
00:12:11,980 --> 00:12:30,210
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, the other thing I just observed that I found kind of interesting in regard to this, is that like, so yeah, that discomfort or whatever feeling, let's say, is involved in care, you can

143
00:12:32,883 --> 00:12:43,387
[SPEAKER_00]: See that that feeling, let's say, or even that thought, or that in that object, could not be there without this body, basically.

144
00:12:43,527 --> 00:12:45,187
[SPEAKER_00]: Or it's as...

145
00:12:45,267 --> 00:12:55,051
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, that can be very... it could be abstract, let's say, but if it's actually being felt or being seen, then that's basically immediately

146
00:12:56,425 --> 00:12:59,348
[SPEAKER_00]: Kind of undermining the whole basis of the care.

147
00:12:59,488 --> 00:13:00,109
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

148
00:13:00,129 --> 00:13:02,111
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's like the contemplation of death.

149
00:13:02,451 --> 00:13:02,891
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

150
00:13:02,951 --> 00:13:12,260
[SPEAKER_01]: Your own death undermines any concern you might have in regard to the world, once you start feeling the the the weight of the contemplation of death, of the liability to it.

151
00:13:12,621 --> 00:13:16,665
[SPEAKER_00]: And the interesting thing is that that initially could seem very

152
00:13:18,203 --> 00:13:25,493
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, yeah, you... when you care about something, you don't want to... you care about it and you don't want to lose it.

153
00:13:26,034 --> 00:13:34,886
[SPEAKER_00]: And the idea of, like, totally abandoning the care is almost, from the point of view of really caring about things, is not pleasant, let's say.

154
00:13:37,142 --> 00:13:50,252
[SPEAKER_00]: But when you reflect that even that care could not exist without this, without like, yeah, the senses, the eyes being, happening to be there, working, the ears happening to work, the mind happening to work normally.

155
00:13:54,296 --> 00:13:57,858
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like, you can't care about it, even if you want to.

156
00:13:57,958 --> 00:13:59,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, inaccessible.

157
00:14:01,220 --> 00:14:03,702
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the another irony, exactly what you'd like.

158
00:14:03,722 --> 00:14:11,747
[SPEAKER_01]: So, from the point of view of care, giving up care is unpleasant, but the only reason you are sort of caring is because it's unpleasant.

159
00:14:12,407 --> 00:14:12,647
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

160
00:14:12,927 --> 00:14:21,673
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already felt unpleasantly, so you care more and you get involved with it, you act further, because it's already unpleasant and that thread of unpleasantness remains, so...

161
00:14:24,051 --> 00:14:30,956
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's also why you have to go forth, basically, at some point.

162
00:14:31,037 --> 00:14:43,767
[SPEAKER_00]: As in other words, it's much... it's harder to do this completely, while still being involved on a day-to-day basis with the things that you've always cared about.

163
00:14:43,787 --> 00:14:45,008
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's what siddhas often say.

164
00:14:45,048 --> 00:14:47,490
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, it's hard to live the holy life unblemished.

165
00:14:48,110 --> 00:14:52,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, free from any blemishes, however subtle, if you're in a household setting.

166
00:14:52,752 --> 00:15:00,997
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yes, you can, again, restrain yourself from coarse things and keep lid on other stuff, but, fundamentally, the whole environment is still tarnishing.

167
00:15:01,497 --> 00:15:03,378
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, you know, it's all about these things.

168
00:15:03,438 --> 00:15:11,842
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all about these ownerships and intimacies and connections and that's just... the way to deal with it is to not be in it.

169
00:15:12,703 --> 00:15:12,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

170
00:15:14,163 --> 00:15:17,425
[SPEAKER_01]: A great deal of impurities will be cleared in that way.

171
00:15:19,860 --> 00:15:20,500
[SPEAKER_02]: This is that sutta.

172
00:15:20,560 --> 00:15:27,262
[SPEAKER_02]: It kind of reminds me of that sutta in Majjhima Nikaya, 87, born of affection.

173
00:15:29,463 --> 00:15:39,405
[SPEAKER_02]: And on one occasion, there was a certain householder whose only son, who was loved by him and pleasing to him, had died.

174
00:15:40,566 --> 00:15:42,746
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of that death, he did not work or eat.

175
00:15:43,327 --> 00:15:46,567
[SPEAKER_02]: He would go to the cemetery and cry, my only son, where are you?

176
00:15:47,168 --> 00:15:48,128
[SPEAKER_02]: My only son, where are you?

177
00:15:48,949 --> 00:15:53,274
[SPEAKER_02]: Then that householder approached the Blessed One, paid respects to Him and sat to one side.

178
00:15:54,455 --> 00:16:00,561
[SPEAKER_02]: When the householder was seated, the Blessed One said to him, householder, you do not appear to have a stable mind.

179
00:16:00,982 --> 00:16:02,103
[SPEAKER_02]: You appear to be deranged.

180
00:16:07,703 --> 00:16:09,784
[SPEAKER_02]: He says, but how could I not be deranged?

181
00:16:10,604 --> 00:16:14,465
[SPEAKER_02]: Bhante, my only son, who I loved and I was pleased by, has died.

182
00:16:15,026 --> 00:16:17,406
[SPEAKER_02]: Because of that death, I do not work or eat.

183
00:16:17,606 --> 00:16:19,187
[SPEAKER_02]: I go to the cemetery and cry.

184
00:16:21,248 --> 00:16:24,969
[SPEAKER_02]: And then the Buddha replies, that's how it is, householder, that's how it is.

185
00:16:25,389 --> 00:16:30,591
[SPEAKER_02]: Householder, sorrow, grief, pain, dejection, anguish are born from affection.

186
00:16:31,371 --> 00:16:33,492
[SPEAKER_02]: They come from affection.

187
00:16:35,053 --> 00:16:35,953
[SPEAKER_02]: I won't go into the rest.

188
00:16:37,617 --> 00:16:39,558
[SPEAKER_02]: But that... because that's the... that's the key point.

189
00:16:44,880 --> 00:16:46,161
[SPEAKER_01]: And what is the affection born of?

190
00:16:49,843 --> 00:16:50,283
[SPEAKER_01]: Ignorance.

191
00:16:51,703 --> 00:16:53,144
[SPEAKER_01]: More... more directly, more specifically.

192
00:16:53,584 --> 00:16:53,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Acting out.

193
00:16:54,805 --> 00:16:55,505
[SPEAKER_02]: More specifically.

194
00:16:55,685 --> 00:16:56,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure.

195
00:16:56,466 --> 00:16:56,806
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure.

196
00:16:57,446 --> 00:16:57,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

197
00:16:58,186 --> 00:17:01,388
[SPEAKER_01]: You would not be affectionate with something that's unpleasant.

198
00:17:01,748 --> 00:17:03,469
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

199
00:17:03,689 --> 00:17:04,930
[SPEAKER_01]: His son was pleasing to him.

200
00:17:07,120 --> 00:17:22,555
[SPEAKER_01]: So, feeling of pleasure has to be as a basis for any form of affection and proliferation and intimacy and dearness.

201
00:17:22,835 --> 00:17:23,636
[SPEAKER_02]: Some people might say, well,

202
00:17:25,257 --> 00:17:35,740
[SPEAKER_02]: I love... or I... if I abandon affection for my mother, my parents, then I don't care for them anymore.

203
00:17:35,920 --> 00:17:37,300
[SPEAKER_02]: You're saying I mustn't care for them.

204
00:17:37,960 --> 00:17:39,601
[SPEAKER_01]: No, I'm saying you must abandon affection.

205
00:17:39,701 --> 00:17:39,861
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

206
00:17:41,121 --> 00:17:43,902
[SPEAKER_02]: But that's like... if I don't have affection...

207
00:17:44,042 --> 00:17:45,762
[SPEAKER_02]: Yes, then the person needs to learn the difference.

208
00:17:45,922 --> 00:17:48,003
[SPEAKER_02]: I just don't help them in any way.

209
00:17:48,443 --> 00:17:49,323
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't think about them.

210
00:17:49,363 --> 00:17:51,604
[SPEAKER_02]: I don't do any duties towards them.

211
00:17:51,804 --> 00:17:52,505
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

212
00:17:52,945 --> 00:17:58,289
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as we just said, that's not... that's not what care is, even... even to begin with.

213
00:17:58,930 --> 00:18:02,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Care is that emotional... being emotionally affected.

214
00:18:03,333 --> 00:18:04,194
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what care means.

215
00:18:05,415 --> 00:18:10,159
[SPEAKER_01]: And you abandon that, and you can still... just help people and act.

216
00:18:10,739 --> 00:18:17,545
[SPEAKER_01]: People can't distinguish that, obviously, because, you know, they most likely don't even keep the precepts, let alone tame their mind on a higher grade.

217
00:18:18,446 --> 00:18:20,147
[SPEAKER_02]: Because it's either affection or hate.

218
00:18:21,266 --> 00:18:25,327
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, nothing in between is seen, because that's, yeah, that's the modes of the mind.

219
00:18:26,167 --> 00:18:30,988
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the kind of categorical modes the mind basically hardened into.

220
00:18:31,248 --> 00:18:32,948
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's either this or that.

221
00:18:33,308 --> 00:18:36,989
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, okay, sure, but I'm telling you, there is something in between.

222
00:18:37,849 --> 00:18:47,791
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you're out of faith, practice the Buddha's teaching and start taming your mind through the gradual training, that whole domain of in-between will become apparent.

223
00:18:48,570 --> 00:18:51,431
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you can understand how can you have intention without craving?

224
00:18:52,231 --> 00:18:57,133
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you care for your parents without feeling anything towards them?

225
00:18:58,513 --> 00:19:02,895
[SPEAKER_01]: But you can't understand why you're still at the bottom of the mountain, in a swamp of sensuality.

226
00:19:02,915 --> 00:19:03,735
[SPEAKER_02]: It's just impossible.

227
00:19:05,416 --> 00:19:07,456
[SPEAKER_01]: You can say, no, no, there's nothing at the top there.

228
00:19:07,636 --> 00:19:08,657
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, how would you know?

229
00:19:09,157 --> 00:19:09,637
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not there.

230
00:19:15,199 --> 00:19:18,480
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's in a way similar to seeing the difference between feeling and craving.

231
00:19:19,522 --> 00:19:28,828
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, yeah, all of these nuances, so to speak, would become... because it all exactly comes down to the feeling not being seen as impermanent.

232
00:19:29,928 --> 00:19:35,071
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling not being understood as suffering, just in and of itself, even if it's a pleasant feeling.

233
00:19:35,091 --> 00:19:36,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Just presence of feeling means suffering.

234
00:19:40,234 --> 00:19:42,736
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly how craving is, in regard to feeling.

235
00:19:42,756 --> 00:19:45,177
[SPEAKER_01]: By not understanding feeling, you crave, when felt.

236
00:19:46,218 --> 00:19:46,598
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

